  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1011 PM CST MON NOV 9 2026\n\nWe were treated to a pleasant November day across Central Alabama\ntoday with high temps settling in the mid 70s under mostly sunny\nskies. We won't be quite as cold tonight with lows dropping into\nthe low to mid 40s. Water vapor imagery this evening reveals a\nsouthern stream trough over Texas that will head our way through\nthe middle of the week. If we peek far enough west, we can see a\nstronger upper low begin to make its way towards the Pacific\nNorthwest. This southern stream system will pass through the area\nlate Tuesday through Wednesday, bringing some mid and upper level\nclouds to the region. We will continue with a low chance for\nlight rain/sprinkles (potentially virga) as guidance can often\nunderdo QPF in these scenarios.\n\nThe stronger northern-stream shortwave trough will bring a\nclipper-like system and strong cold front into the area Wednesday\nnight into Thursday. Low-level moisture return will be lacking\nahead of the front with dew points mainly in the low to mid 50s,\nbut strong forcing and steep mid-level lapse rates will be\nsufficient for a few showers Wednesday evening into Thursday\nmorning. There may be just enough instability for an isolated\nthunderstorm across our northern areas, but severe storms are not\nexpected at this time. A much cooler air mass moves in behind the\nfront, with highs in the 50s and high chances for the first\nfreeze of the season across at least northern portions of the\narea. Latest NBM probabilities maintain a 60-90% chance for\nfreezing temps across the northern half of the CWA.\n\n95/Castillo",
